    <statement><p>Verify that <m>\mathbf{F}=\nabla\varphi</m> and evaluate the line integral of
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> over the given path:</p>
<p><m>\mathbf{F} = \left\lt x^{5}y^{6},x^{6}y^{5}\right&gt;</m> , <m>\quad \varphi(x,y)=\frac1{6}x^{6}y^{6}</m> ;</p>
<p>Upper half of a circle with radius <m>11</m>, centered at the origin and oriented counterclockwise</p>
<p>Answer : <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\varphi ( x,y )=\frac1{6}x^{6}y^{6}</m>:
<me>\nabla \varphi = \left\lt  \frac{\partial \varphi }{\partial x},\frac{\partial \varphi }{\partial y} \right&gt; = \left\lt  x^{5}y^{6}, x^{6}y^{5} \right&gt; =\mathbf{F}</me> 
We now use the Fundamental Theorem of Gradient Vector Fields. 

 The terminal point is <m>Q=( -11,0 )</m> and the initial point is <m>P=( 11,0 )</m>, therefore:
<me>\int_{\mathbf{c}} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{s}  =
\varphi (Q)-\varphi (P)=</me><me>\varphi ( -11,0 )-\varphi ( 11,0 )=
\frac1{6}\cdot (-11)^{6}\cdot 0^{6}-\frac1{6}\cdot 11^{6}\cdot 0^{6} = 0</me>
